# Copy to /etc/hivepilot/shared.env and fill in the vars that must apply to
# EVERY hivepilot-*.service unit, then chmod 600 it (it may hold secrets).
#
# WHY this file exists: same env-silo lesson as deploy/openrc/conf.d/
# shared.env.example -- each hivepilot-*.service unit loads its OWN
# `/etc/hivepilot/hivepilot-<svc>.env` file only. A var set in only SOME of
# those files is invisible to the others. This bit a real deployment:
# IS_SANDBOX=1 was set for hivepilot-api and hivepilot-scheduler but NOT
# hivepilot-telegram, and since a Telegram approval callback RESUMES the
# pipeline INSIDE the telegram process, the developer stage ran without
# IS_SANDBOX and failed. See deploy/systemd/README.md and
# deploy/openrc/README.md, "Env silos -- read this".
#
# Rule of thumb: anything a RESUMED pipeline needs (any approval-callback
# path -- Telegram/Slack/Discord) must live here, not in a single service's
# own env file, because you cannot predict in advance which service process
# will be the one that resumes a given run.
#
# FORMAT WARNING: systemd's EnvironmentFile= does NOT run a shell. There is
# no `export ` keyword and no `$VAR`/`${VAR}` expansion here -- every line
# below must be a plain KEY=value pair (see deploy/systemd/README.md,
# "EnvironmentFile format" for the full explanation and what happens if you
# copy an OpenRC conf.d file verbatim -- `export FOO=bar` is stored as a
# literal env var named `export FOO` with value `bar`, not what you meant).

# IS_SANDBOX=1
# HIVEPILOT_CLAUDE_CAPTURE_USAGE=false
